-- title:
--   Regression function $f^\star(a)=\mathbb E[y\mid x=a]$
-- statement:
--   For a distribution on $\mathcal X\times\mathbb R$ with integrable outcome, $f^\star$ is the **regression function** if
--
--   $$
--   f^\star(a)=\mathbb E[y\mid x=a].
--   $$
--
--   It is the minimizer of $L$ among measurable predictors for the square loss.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mathbb E[y\mid x]$ is the conditional expectation given the $\sigma$-algebra generated by $x$, and $f^\star(x)$ equals it almost surely. Integrability of the outcome is part of the definition, because Lean's conditional expectation of a non-integrable function is the junk $0$.
-- source:
--   Foster & Rakhlin, Foundations of Reinforcement Learning and Interactive Decision Making, arXiv:2312.16730v1, Definition 1, p. 9

import Mathlib
open MeasureTheory

namespace FoundationsRL.StatLearning

/-- **`f⋆` is the regression function** `f⋆(a) := E[y | x = a]` of the distribution `M` on `X × ℝ`.
Foster & Rakhlin, *Foundations of Reinforcement Learning and Interactive Decision Making*, arXiv:2312.16730v1, Definition 1, p. 9.

**Formalization Note.** `E[y | x]` is Mathlib's conditional expectation with respect to the
σ-algebra generated by the feature `x`, and `f⋆` is a version of it: `f⋆(x) = E[y | x]` holds
`M`-almost surely. The outcome is required to be integrable, since Lean's conditional expectation of
a non-integrable function is the junk value `0`. -/
def IsRegressionFunction {X : Type*} [mX : MeasurableSpace X] (M : Measure (X × ℝ))
    (fstar : X → ℝ) : Prop :=
  Integrable (fun z : X × ℝ => z.2) M ∧
    (fun z : X × ℝ => fstar z.1) =ᵐ[M]
      M[fun z : X × ℝ => z.2 | MeasurableSpace.comap Prod.fst mX]

end FoundationsRL.StatLearning


